const title = "sf-intelligence 0.3.1 - 39 defects in shipped tools, and the 18 the fixes caused";
const description =
  "What shipped in sf-intelligence 0.3.1: 39 verified defects fixed in already-shipped tools, the 18 regressions the fixes introduced, and the gates that now catch both.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What kind of release is sf-intelligence 0.3.1?",
    a: "A correctness release against tools that were already shipped. No new capability tier. Three adversarial audits went through the permissions, schema-and-search, and automation tool families with an explicit instruction to hunt only tools that predated the work, and produced 39 verified defects - each one reproduced against a real org vault, measured for scale, and checked against a sibling tool that answered the same question. Every fix carries a test proven to fail before the change, and the numbers quoted in these notes were re-measured after the merge rather than carried over from the design.",
  },
  {
    q: "Why did sf-intelligence report most of an org's Apex as dead code?",
    a: "Because the reachability walk only followed callsApex edges. A class reached through async dispatch, a static-field reference, or a Visualforce controller binding looked unreachable, and the soundness block attached complete: true to that verdict because it was derived from a dynamic-Apex signal that knew nothing about which edge types had been skipped. On one org it called 85 of 186 classes likely dead, 75 of them test classes. Three tools shared that defect through three separate hardcoded walks. They now share one primitive that treats usage as every incoming edge type except structural and access edges - a deny-list, because an allow-list is what caused the bug: it was written before dispatchesAsync existed and could never learn about it. The same org now reports zero.",
  },
  {
    q: "Why did a Salesforce permission still read as granted after it was removed?",
    a: "Because a vault can hold two complete retrievals of the same org, and the importer let the older one win. Node writes are INSERT OR REPLACE keyed on component id, and the directory walk is alphabetical, so a copy under the SFDX layout was written first and a stale flat copy overwrote it. On a real vault three profiles reported a multi-factor-authentication bypass that the current retrieval no longer declared. The canonical layout now wins, and any component present at more than one path with differing content carries a sourceConflict field naming every path, which copy answered, and how that copy was chosen. Where the vault cannot establish which retrieval is current it says precedence is undetermined rather than guessing - retrieval timestamps are recorded per metadata family, never per path, so a newest-wins rule would be a guess wearing a timestamp.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the advertised-versus-enforced schema gate in sf-intelligence?",
    a: "A test that asserts every tool's advertised JSON Schema matches the validator that actually runs, across all 217 registered tools, on four axes: the property set, which keys are genuinely required, whether a strict validator advertises additionalProperties false, and enum membership in both directions. Required is measured by removal rather than by reading optionality off the shape, because a key can be optional to a caller who supplies an alias instead. It found 45 disagreements. Eighteen were introduced by this release's own work and are fixed; the remaining 27 predate it and are baselined with a written reason each, and the baseline fails if an entry goes stale or a known drift widens.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does a paginated sf-intelligence tool return every row it says it has?",
    a: "It does now, and two tools did not before. A resume pointer was computed from what a handler intended to return rather than what survived a second trim applied further down, so following it skipped whole windows: one tool claimed 647 findings and 94 were reachable, another claimed 570 and 542 were reachable. A pointer is now corrected only on a positive match against a page size the payload itself publishes, and invalidated with a stated reason otherwise. Separately, a search that gained pagination in this release shipped without a total order on its sort, so a tied name let one row appear on two pages and a sibling on none - fixed, and the rule written at the query.",
  },
];

